<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herero people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ovaherero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Herero are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>-herding people of central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>. In the 19th century they contested land and herds with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oorlam">Oorlam</a> and Nama, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharero">Maharero</a> won their independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonker-afrikaner">Jonker Afrikaner</a>'s successors in the 1860s. Their rising against German rule in 1904 was crushed by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothar-von-trotha">Lothar von Trotha</a> in a campaign widely recognised as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a> of the 20th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herero-people.jpg" alt="Herero people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bries · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herero_women.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
